    {
      "content": "The Veinroot displays a bulbous central mass of fibrous tissue with\n reddish branching structures resembling exposed veins.",
      "left": "202px",
      "top": "332px",
      "scale": "1",
      "rotate": "0",
      "fontFamily": "melonfruit",
      "fontSize": "28px",
      "color": "rgb(17, 17, 17)"
    },
    {
      "content": "Its design reveals high vascularization, optimized for mineral fluid transport from saturated soils.",
      "left": "85px",
      "top": "409px",
      "scale": "1",
      "rotate": "0",
      "fontFamily": "melonfruit",
      "fontSize": "28px",
      "color": "rgb(17, 17, 17)"
    },
    {
      "content": "Common in hydrocarbon-rich zones, its reddish sap contains flammable resinous compounds. ",
      "left": "92px",
      "top": "447px",
      "scale": "1",
      "rotate": "0",
      "fontFamily": "melonfruit",
      "fontSize": "28px",
      "color": "rgb(17, 17, 17)"
    },
    {
      "content": "Preliminary studies suggest that some of Maxkodia’s primitive communities use\n it as a natural fuel in fire rituals.",
